     ddrraaiidd
             GUID                  oorrgg..ooppeennzzffss::ddrraaiidd
             READ-ONLY COMPATIBLE  no

             This feature enables use of the ddrraaiidd vdev type.  dRAID is a
             variant of RAID-Z which provides integrated distributed hot
             spares that allow faster resilvering while retaining the benefits
             of RAID-Z.  Data, parity, and spare space are organized in redun‐
             dancy groups and distributed evenly over all of the devices.

             This feature becomes aaccttiivvee when creating a pool which uses the
             ddrraaiidd vdev type, or when adding a new ddrraaiidd vdev to an existing
             pool.

     eeddoonnrr
             GUID                  oorrgg..iilllluummooss::eeddoonnrr
             DEPENDENCIES          eexxtteennssiibbllee__ddaattaasseett
             READ-ONLY COMPATIBLE  no

             This feature enables the use of the Edon-R hash algorithm for
             checksum, including for nopwrite (if compression is also enabled,
             an overwrite of a block whose checksum matches the data being
             written will be ignored).  In an abundance of caution, Edon-R re‐
             quires verification when used with dedup: zzffss sseett
             ddeedduupp=eeddoonnrr,vveerriiffyy (see zfs-set(8)).

             Edon-R is a very high-performance hash algorithm that was part of
             the NIST SHA-3 competition.  It provides extremely high hash per‐
             formance (over 350% faster than SHA-256), but was not selected
             because of its unsuitability as a general purpose secure hash al‐
             gorithm.  This implementation utilizes the new salted checksum‐
             ming functionality in ZFS, which means that the checksum is pre-
             seeded with a secret 256-bit random key (stored on the pool) be‐
             fore being fed the data block to be checksummed.  Thus the pro‐
             duced checksums are unique to a given pool, preventing hash col‐
             lision attacks on systems with dedup.

             When the eeddoonnrr feature is set to eennaabblleedd, the administrator can
             turn on the eeddoonnrr checksum on any dataset using zzffss sseett
             cchheecckkssuumm=eeddoonnrr _d_s_e_t (see zfs-set(8)).  This feature becomes
             aaccttiivvee once a cchheecckkssuumm property has been set to eeddoonnrr, and will
             return to being eennaabblleedd once all filesystems that have ever had
